Step 1: Check prerequisites
Make sure these are running:
- Jenkins: http://localhost:8080
- Docker Desktop: open and running
- GitHub repository: https://github.com/sahilkazi1010/shopease
Step 2: Add a Jenkinsfile to your GitHub repository
Open your shopease repository on GitHub, click Add file → Create new file, and name it exactly:

Jenkinsfile

Paste this code:

pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                git branch: 'main',
                    url: 'https://github.com/sahilkazi1010/shopease.git'
            }
        }

        stage('Build Docker Image') {
            steps {
                bat 'docker build -t shopease .'
            }
        }

        stage('Deploy Using Docker') {
            steps {
                bat 'docker rm -f shopease-container || exit /b 0'
                bat 'docker run -d --name shopease-container -p 8082:80 shopease'
            }
        }
    }
}



Click Commit changes to save the file to the main branch.

Important: This example assumes Jenkins runs on Windows and can access Docker Desktop. If Jenkins runs as a Windows service, make sure its service account can access Docker. If you run Jenkins in a Linux container, the commands and Docker access setup will be different.
Step 3: Create a Jenkins pipeline
1. Open http://localhost:8080.
2. Click New Item.
3. Enter the name Docker_Deploy_Pipeline.
4. Select Pipeline and click OK.
5. Scroll to the Pipeline section.
6. Set Definition to Pipeline script from SCM.
7. Set SCM to Git.
8. Enter the repository URL:
   
   https://github.com/sahilkazi1010/shopease.git
9. Set Branch Specifier to */main.
10. Set Script Path to Jenkinsfile.
11. Click Save.
Step 4: Run the pipeline
1. Open Docker_Deploy_Pipeline.
2. Click Build Now.
3. Click the build number under Build History.
4. Select Console Output.
5. Check that the Checkout, Build Docker Image, and Deploy Using Docker stages complete successfully.
Step 5: Verify the deployment
Open Command Prompt and run:
docker ps


You should see a container named shopease-container.

Open these URLs in your browser:
- Main page: http://localhost:8082
- Product page: http://localhost:8082/product.html
Step 6: Practical result
Result: Successfully created a Jenkins pipeline that retrieves a Jenkinsfile from a remote GitHub repository, builds a Docker image of the web application, and deploys the application inside a Docker container.

If the build fails, send me a screenshot of the Console Output. We'll fix the exact error rather than changing everything at once.
